<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="unit3-summary">
  <title>Unit 3 Summary</title>

   <subsection xml:id="unit3-summary-objectives">
    <title>Unit 3 Learning Objectives</title>

  <p>In Unit 3, we covered the following Learning Objectives:</p>
   <ul>
    <li><p>3.1 Lists
        <ul>
          <li>3.1.A Develop code that stores multiple related values in a single variable.</li>
          <li>3.1.B Develop code to manipulate a list, and determine the result produced.</li>
          <li>3.1.C Develop code using iteration statements to traverse a list, and determine the result produced.</li>
          <li>3.1.D Explain how the use of data abstraction manages complexity in program code.</li>
        </ul>
        </p>
    </li>
    <li><p>3.2 Procedures
        <ul>
          <li>3.2.A Develop code to define procedures with and without parameters.</li>
            <li>3.2.B Develop code for procedure calls, and determine the result of procedure calls.</li>
            <li>3.2.C Explain how the use of procedural abstraction manages complexity in a program.</li>
        </ul>
        </p>
    </li>
    <li><p>3.3 Using Existing Code and Libraries
        <ul>
          <li>3.3.A Develop code using appropriate libraries or existing code segments to create a program.</li>
        </ul>
        </p>
    </li>
   </ul>
   </subsection>

   <subsection xml:id="unit3-summary-vocab">
    <title>Unit 3 Vocabulary</title>

  <p>Review the following Unit 3 vocabulary:</p>

     <activity label="vocab-review-3-1-lists">
      <title>3.1 Lists Vocabulary</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Review the vocabulary in 3.1. Drag the vocabulary term from the left and drop it
          on its correct definition on the right.
          Click the "Check Me" button to see if you are correct.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <feedback>
        <p>Review the vocabulary in 3.1 and try again.</p>
      </feedback>

      <cardsort>
        <match>
          <premise>data collection</premise>
          <response>a set of data, organized and stored together</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>list</premise>
          <response>an ordered collection of values stored under a single variable name</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>index</premise>
          <response>the position of an element in a list, starting from 0</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>element</premise>
          <response>a single value in a list</response>
        </match>
        <match> 
          <premise>traversal</premise>
          <response>stepping through each element in a list, often using a loop</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>linear search</premise>
          <response>checking each element in a list one by one until the desired value is found or the end of the list is reached</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>partial traversal</premise>
          <response>stepping through only some elements in a list, often stopping early when a goal is reached</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>data abstraction</premise>
          <response>hiding the complex implementation details of a data structure and showing only the needed features to the user</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>refactor</premise>
          <response>the process of rewriting existing code without changing its functionality</response>
        </match>
      </cardsort>
      </activity>

 <activity label="vocab-review-3-2-functions">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Review the vocabulary in 3.2 Functions. Drag the vocabulary term from the left and drop it
          on its correct definition on the right.
          Click the "Check Me" button to see if you are correct.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <feedback>
        <p>Review the vocabulary in 3.2 Functions and try again.</p>
      </feedback>

      <cardsort>
        <match>
          <premise>procedure/function</premise>
          <response>a named group of programming instructions that perform a specific task</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>procedural abstraction</premise>
          <response>grouping related code into procedures that can be called by name, hiding detailed implementationsteps from the rest of the program</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>modularity</premise>
          <response>the subdivision of a program into smaller, independent procedures or components</response> 
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>procedure/function definition</premise>
          <response>the code that defines what a procedure or function does</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>procedure/function call</premise>
          <response>the code that runs a procedure or function</response>
          </match>
        <match>
          <premise>parameters</premise>
          <response>variables that are defined in a procedure or function and used to pass in data</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>arguments</premise>
          <response>values that are passed to a procedure or function when it is called</response>
        </match>
      </cardsort>
    </activity>

      <activity label="vocab-review-3-3-libraries">
      <statement>
        <p>
           Review the vocabulary in 3.3 Libraries. Drag the vocabulary term from the left and drop it
          on its correct definition on the right.
          Click the "Check Me" button to see if you are correct.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <feedback>
        <p>Review the vocabulary in 3.3 Libraries and try again.</p>
      </feedback>

      <cardsort>
        <match>
          <premise>library</premise>
          <response>A collection of classes written by other programmers</response>
          
        </match>

        <match>
          <premise>import</premise>
          <response>To include a library in your code</response>
        </match>

        <match>
          <premise>API</premise>
          <response>The rules or specification on how to use a library</response>
        </match>

        <match>
          <premise>module</premise>
          <response>A single Python file that can be imported into another program</response>
        </match>

        <match>
          <premise>documentation</premise>
          <response>The written description of a library's usage</response>
        </match>

       
      </cardsort>
    </activity>
  </subsection>
</section>
